**Key idea:** An APB driver implements a non-pipelined bus protocol where each transfer consists of a one-cycle SETUP phase (`psel=1`, `penable=0`), followed by an ACCESS phase (`penable=1`) that must be sustained for at least one clock cycle until `pready` samples high at an active clock edge.

```wavedrom
{ "signal": [
  { "name": "pclk",    "wave": "p....." },
  { "name": "psel",    "wave": "01..0." },
  { "name": "penable", "wave": "0.1.0." },
  { "name": "pwrite",  "wave": "x1..x." },
  { "name": "paddr",   "wave": "x===x.", "data": ["A0", "A0", "A0"] },
  { "name": "pwdata",  "wave": "x===x.", "data": ["D0", "D0", "D0"] },
  { "name": "pready",  "wave": "0.010." }
],
  "head": { "text": "APB Write with 1 Wait State (SETUP -> ACCESS wait -> ACCESS completion -> IDLE)" } }
```

### Complete Code Example
```systemverilog
// 1. Transaction Definition
class apb_seq_item extends uvm_sequence_item;
  rand bit [31:0] addr;
  rand bit [31:0] data;
  rand bit        write;
  rand bit [3:0]  strb;
  bit             slverr;

  `uvm_object_utils_begin(apb_seq_item)
    `uvm_field_int(addr,   UVM_ALL_ON)
    `uvm_field_int(data,   UVM_ALL_ON)
    `uvm_field_int(write,  UVM_ALL_ON)
    `uvm_field_int(strb,   UVM_ALL_ON)
    `uvm_field_int(slverr, UVM_ALL_ON)
  `uvm_object_utils_end

  function new(string name = "apb_seq_item");
    super.new(name);
    strb = 4'b1111;
  endfunction
endclass

// 2. Interface with Defined Clocking Block
interface apb_if(input logic pclk, input logic preset_n);
  logic [31:0] paddr;
  logic        psel;
  logic        penable;
  logic        pwrite;
  logic [3:0]  pstrb;
  logic [31:0] pwdata;
  logic [31:0] prdata;
  logic        pready;
  logic        pslverr;

  // Master clocking block with defined input/output skews
  clocking mck @(posedge pclk);
    default input #1step output #1ns;
    output paddr, psel, penable, pwrite, pstrb, pwdata;
    input  prdata, pready, pslverr;
  endclocking
endinterface

// 3. Driver Implementation
class apb_driver extends uvm_driver #(apb_seq_item);
  `uvm_component_utils(apb_driver)
  virtual apb_if vif;

  function new(string name, uvm_component parent);
    super.new(name, parent);
  endfunction

  virtual function void build_phase(uvm_phase phase);
    super.build_phase(phase);
    if (!uvm_config_db#(virtual apb_if)::get(this, "", "vif", vif))
      `uvm_fatal("DRV_NO_VIF", "Virtual interface vif not found in uvm_config_db")
  endfunction

  virtual task run_phase(uvm_phase phase);
    forever begin
      reset_signals();
      // Level-sensitive check: avoid hanging if reset is already deasserted (high)
      if (!vif.preset_n)
        @(posedge vif.preset_n);

      // Concurrent driving and asynchronous reset abort/recovery
      fork
        begin : drive_loop
          forever begin
            seq_item_port.get_next_item(req);
            drive_transfer(req);
            seq_item_port.item_done();
          end
        end
        begin : reset_monitor
          @(negedge vif.preset_n);
        end
      join_any
      disable fork; // Abort in-flight transfer immediately on reset assertion
    end
  endtask

  virtual task drive_transfer(apb_seq_item item);
    // 1. SETUP Phase: Assert PSEL, drive control/address/data, hold PENABLE low
    @(vif.mck);
    vif.mck.psel    <= 1'b1;
    vif.mck.penable <= 1'b0;
    vif.mck.paddr   <= item.addr;
    vif.mck.pwrite  <= item.write;
    vif.mck.pstrb   <= item.strb;
    if (item.write) vif.mck.pwdata <= item.data;

    // 2. ACCESS Phase: Assert PENABLE
    @(vif.mck);
    vif.mck.penable <= 1'b1;

    // 3. Force at least one ACCESS sampling edge: wait until PREADY is high
    @(vif.mck);
    while (!vif.mck.pready) begin
      @(vif.mck);
    end

    // 4. Sample read data and error response at completion edge
    if (!item.write) item.data = vif.mck.prdata;
    item.slverr = vif.mck.pslverr;

    // 5. Deassert PSEL and PENABLE to return to IDLE (or transition to next SETUP)
    vif.mck.psel    <= 1'b0;
    vif.mck.penable <= 1'b0;
  endtask

  virtual task reset_signals();
    vif.mck.psel    <= 1'b0;
    vif.mck.penable <= 1'b0;
    vif.mck.paddr   <= '0;
    vif.mck.pwrite  <= 1'b0;
    vif.mck.pstrb   <= '0;
    vif.mck.pwdata  <= '0;
  endtask
endclass
```

### Explanation
- **Clocking Block Synchronization:** Using `vif.mck` eliminates race conditions by driving signals with defined output skew (`#1ns`) and sampling inputs in the Preponed region (`#1step`).
- **ACCESS Sampling Edge:** Scheduling `penable <= 1` at clock edge $T$ means the ACCESS phase becomes active during cycle $T \to T+1$. The driver advances to clock edge $T+1$ before sampling `pready`. If wait states occur (`pready == 0`), it waits on subsequent clock edges until `pready` samples high.
- **Robust Reset Handling:** Checking `if (!vif.preset_n) @(posedge vif.preset_n);` prevents deadlocks if `preset_n` is already high before `run_phase` begins.
- **Abort & Reset Recovery:** The `fork...join_any` construct monitors `@(negedge vif.preset_n)` concurrently with `drive_loop`. If reset fires mid-transfer, `disable fork` terminates the active transfer, calls `reset_signals()`, and waits for the next reset release.
- **Limitation:** In this standard driver model, a transaction aborted mid-transfer by reset does not call `item_done()`, so the sequence must restart or handle sequence-item re-arbitration upon reset recovery.

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- **Premature PREADY Sampling:** Evaluating `pready` in the same clock edge where `penable` was asserted samples the bus before the slave has even entered the ACCESS phase, corrupting wait-state handling.
- **Signal Glitching in ACCESS:** Modifying `paddr` or `pwdata` while `penable=1` and `pready=0` violates the AMBA APB specification; signals must remain stable until `pready` samples high.

### Follow-ups
- How to support back-to-back transfers without an IDLE cycle? Transition directly from ACCESS completion to the SETUP phase of the next item without deasserting `psel`.
- What additional signals are in APB4? APB4 introduces `pprot` (protection type) and `pstrb` (byte write strobes).

