**Key idea:** An AHB-Lite driver is best implemented as a single-threaded, cycle-based state machine where the Address Phase of transfer $N$ overlaps the Data Phase of transfer $N-1$; the claim that pipelining requires concurrent threads is false and introduces illegal overlapping sequencer calls.

```wavedrom
{ "signal": [
  { "name": "HCLK",    "wave": "p....." },
  { "name": "HREADY",  "wave": "1....." },
  { "name": "HTRANS",  "wave": "=.===.", "data": ["NSEQ", "SEQ", "IDLE"] },
  { "name": "HADDR",   "wave": "x.===.", "data": ["A0", "A1", "A2"] },
  { "name": "HWRITE",  "wave": "x.===.", "data": ["Wr", "Rd", "Rd"] },
  { "name": "HWDATA",  "wave": "x..==.", "data": ["D0 (A0)", "D1 (A1)"] }
],
  "head": { "text": "AHB-Lite Pipelined Bus: Address Phase N overlaps Data Phase N-1" } }
```

### Pseudocode (Cycle-Based AHB-Lite Driver Architecture)
```systemverilog
// NOTE: Architectural pseudocode illustrating single-owner cycle-based AHB-Lite pipelining.
// Not a standalone compilable package.

class ahb_driver_pseudocode extends uvm_driver #(ahb_seq_item);
  `uvm_component_utils(ahb_driver_pseudocode)
  virtual ahb_if vif;

  // Pipeline stage tracking
  ahb_seq_item addr_item = null;  // Item currently in Address Phase
  ahb_seq_item data_item = null;  // Item currently in Data Phase
  bit in_error_cycle_1   = 0;     // Tracks 2-cycle HRESP error state
  bit should_cancel_addr = 0;

  virtual task run_phase(uvm_phase phase);
    reset_signals();
    if (!vif.hreset_n) @(posedge vif.hreset_n);

    // Single bus owner clock loop: handles address & data phases synchronously
    forever @(posedge vif.hclk) begin
      if (!vif.hreset_n) begin
        reset_signals();
        addr_item = null;
        data_item = null;
        in_error_cycle_1 = 0;
        should_cancel_addr = 0;
        continue;
      end

      // -------------------------------------------------------------
      // 1. Wait-State Handling (HREADY Freeze)
      // -------------------------------------------------------------
      if (!vif.hready) begin
        // When slave inserts wait states, FREEZE all bus signals.
        // Both address and data phase outputs must remain completely stable.
        if (vif.hresp == HRESP_ERROR)
          in_error_cycle_1 = 1; // First cycle of 2-cycle error response
        continue;
      end

      // -------------------------------------------------------------
      // 2. Data Phase Retirement (for data_item)
      // -------------------------------------------------------------
      if (data_item != null) begin
        if (in_error_cycle_1 && vif.hresp == HRESP_ERROR) begin
          // Second cycle of 2-cycle HRESP ERROR completes
          data_item.resp = HRESP_ERROR;
          in_error_cycle_1 = 0;
          // Address phase of next transfer must be canceled via IDLE
          should_cancel_addr = 1;
        end else begin
          if (!data_item.write)
            data_item.rdata = vif.hrdata;
          data_item.resp = vif.hresp;
        end

        // Legal sequencer response separation: retire item when data phase completes
        seq_item_port.item_done(data_item);
        data_item = null;
      end

      // -------------------------------------------------------------
      // 3. Advance Address Phase to Data Phase
      // -------------------------------------------------------------
      if (addr_item != null) begin
        data_item = addr_item;
        addr_item = null;
        // Drive HWDATA during data phase for write transfers
        if (data_item.write)
          vif.hwdata <= data_item.wdata;
        else
          vif.hwdata <= '0;
      end else begin
        vif.hwdata <= '0;
      end

      // -------------------------------------------------------------
      // 4. Drive New Address Phase (Non-blocking sequencer poll)
      // -------------------------------------------------------------
      if (should_cancel_addr) begin
        // Cancel next transfer due to slave error response
        vif.htrans <= HTRANS_IDLE;
        should_cancel_addr = 0;
      end else begin
        // Poll sequencer without blocking or issuing concurrent get_next_item calls
        seq_item_port.try_next_item(req);
        if (req != null) begin
          addr_item   = req;
          vif.haddr  <= addr_item.addr;
          vif.htrans <= HTRANS_NONSEQ;
          vif.hwrite <= addr_item.write;
          vif.hsize  <= addr_item.size;
          vif.hburst <= addr_item.burst;
        end else begin
          // Pipeline retirement: no stimulus available, drive IDLE transfer
          vif.htrans <= HTRANS_IDLE;
          // Hold HADDR stable to minimize bus toggling
        end
      end
    end
  endtask

  virtual task reset_signals();
    vif.htrans <= HTRANS_IDLE;
    vif.haddr  <= '0;
    vif.hwrite <= 1'b0;
    vif.hwdata <= '0;
  endtask
endclass
```

### Explanation
- **Why Concurrent Threads Are Flawed:** Spawning two parallel `pipeline_worker` threads that each call `get_next_item()` is illegal in standard UVM: multiple simultaneous `get_next_item()` calls on the same `seq_item_port` corrupt sequencer arbitration and race against each other when `HREADY` deasserts. Pipelining does not require concurrent threads.
- **Single Bus Owner:** A single loop synchronous to `@(posedge vif.hclk)` explicitly tracks the pipeline state (`addr_item` and `data_item`). This eliminates thread synchronization semaphores and prevents race conditions.
- **HREADY Freeze:** When `vif.hready == 0`, the entire bus freezes. The driver preserves `HADDR`, `HTRANS`, `HWRITE`, and `HWDATA` without advancing the pipeline until `HREADY` returns high.
- **Two-Cycle HRESP ERROR Handling:** An AHB slave signals an error using a mandatory two-cycle sequence: Cycle 1 (`HRESP=ERROR, HREADY=0`) and Cycle 2 (`HRESP=ERROR, HREADY=1`). On Cycle 2, the master must cancel the next transfer by forcing `HTRANS = IDLE`.
- **Sequencer Handshake:** Items are retrieved with `try_next_item()` and retired with `item_done(data_item)` only after their data phase concludes, maintaining strict request/response separation.

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- **Pipeline Retirement:** When the sequence stops sending items, the driver must drive `HTRANS = IDLE` in the address phase while allowing the active data phase of the final item to complete cleanly.
- **Two-Cycle Error Cancellation:** Failing to force `HTRANS=IDLE` during the second cycle of an error response causes the slave to execute an unintended transaction.

### Follow-ups
- How to handle sequential bursts? Set `HTRANS = HTRANS_SEQ` on beats after the initial `NONSEQ` transfer.
- Can `get_next_item` be used instead of `try_next_item`? Only if the sequence guarantees uninterrupted back-to-back traffic; `try_next_item` is preferred because it handles idle pipeline bubbles gracefully.

